    {
      "kind": "Figure",
      "label": "Figure 1",
      "description": "Four-qubit Hopf tree in the first-paper convention. A left branch contributes $`\\cos\\theta_{j}`$ and a right branch contributes $`\\sin\\theta_{j}`$ . The internal labels are the magnitude coordinates, the blue labels beneath the leaves are the complex phase coordinates, and the circled numbers give the native gate-application order. Padded binary labels identify subtree anchors and leaves.",
      "source_id": "A1.SS2.fig1",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS2.fig1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "Figure",
      "label": "Figure 2",
      "description": "Native four-qubit Hopf ansatz circuit. For $`\\mathrm{HopfReal}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{\\mathrm{mag}})`$ , every box is $`R_{y}(\\theta_{j})`$ . For $`\\mathrm{HopfComplex}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})`$ , each final-layer gate $`j=8+r`$ , $`0\\leq r<8`$ , is promoted to $`R_{\\mathbb{C}}(\\theta_{8+r},\\theta_{16+2r},\\theta_{17+2r})`$ . The blue boxes identify that promoted final layer.",
      "source_id": "A1.SS2.fig2",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS2.fig2"
    },
    {
      "kind": "Table",
      "label": "Table I",
      "description": "Four-qubit internal-node registry. For $`j=2^{d}+r`$ , the checkpoint prefix is $`r`$ , its target is $`q_{4-d}`$ , $`\\ell_{0}(j)`$ is the left-child anchor, and $`\\lambda(j)`$ addresses the normalized local complement.",
      "source_id": "A1.SS3.fig1",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS3.fig1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "Figure",
      "label": "Figure 3",
      "description": "Four-qubit depth completion. The left bracket is $`A_{2}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{\\mathrm{mag}})=U_{2}U_{1}U_{0}`$ and the right bracket is $`B_{2}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{\\mathrm{mag}})=U_{3}`$ , giving the full-unitary factorization $`U_{\\mathrm{chk}}=B_{2}A_{2}`$ . Open controls select zero-valued prefix bits. Section A.8 constructs the corresponding complex depth- $`2`$ completion.",
      "source_id": "A1.SS4.fig1",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS4.fig1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "Figure",
      "label": "Figure 4",
      "description": "Fully addressed four-qubit real-magnitude frame $`W_{\\mathbb{R}}`$ , with $`V_{0}`$ acting first. Every non-target qubit is fixed by a closed or open control, so each gate preserves the required state-and-complement column action. A box labeled $`\\theta_{j}`$ denotes Eq. ( 7 ). The same addressed gate skeleton is used for the complex-magnitude frame constructed in Sec. A.6 . Circuit layouts use the visual language of the quantum-circuit-drawio library [ 48 ] .",
      "source_id": "A1.SS5.fig1",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS5.fig1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "Figure",
      "label": "Figure 5",
      "description": "Real-Hopf global-gradient circuit. The forward bracket uses the state-column contract $`\\mathrm{HopfReal}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{\\mathrm{mag}})\\ket{0000}=W_{\\mathbb{R}}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{\\mathrm{mag}})\\ket{0000}`$ . The reverse block is $`W_{\\mathbb{R}}^{\\dagger}`$ . The measured bits are $`b_{m}`$ and $`\\boldsymbol{y}=(y_{1},y_{2},y_{3},y_{4})`$ .",
      "source_id": "A1.SS7.fig1",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS7.fig1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "Figure",
      "label": "Figure 6",
      "description": "Complex-Hopf magnitude circuit. The forward bracket uses the state-column chain in Eq. ( 61 ). The reverse bracket uses the full-unitary identity $`W_{\\mathbb{C}}^{\\dagger}=W_{\\mathbb{R}}^{\\dagger}D_{\\mathrm{ph}}^{\\dagger}`$ , so the physical order is $`D_{\\mathrm{ph}}^{\\dagger}`$ followed by $`W_{\\mathbb{R}}^{\\dagger}`$ . The addressed $`R_{\\mathbb{C}}`$ compiler realizes the same decoder up to the compiler global phase in Eq. ( 63 ).",
      "source_id": "A1.SS7.fig2",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS7.fig2"
    },
    {
      "kind": "Figure",
      "label": "Figure 7",
      "description": "Direct complex phase circuit. Native $`\\mathrm{HopfComplex}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})`$ prepares the state through the state-column contract in Eq. ( 61 ). After the controlled reflection, the system is measured in $`Z`$ and the branch ancilla in $`Y`$ , implemented by $`S^{\\dagger}`$ followed by $`H`$ . This circuit uses the leaf-local phase record directly.",
      "source_id": "A1.SS7.fig3",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS7.fig3"
    },
    {
      "kind": "Table",
      "label": "Table II",
      "description": "All four real checkpoint settings. Compiler charges are consolidated in Table A.9 .",
      "source_id": "A1.SS8.fig1",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS8.fig1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "Figure",
      "label": "Figure 8",
      "description": "Real-Hopf depth- $`2`$ checkpoint. The forward bracket uses the state-column contract $`\\mathrm{HopfReal}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{\\mathrm{mag}})\\ket{0000}=B_{2}A_{2}\\ket{0000}`$ . After controlled- $`O`$ , the reverse suffix is $`B_{2}^{\\dagger}=U_{3}^{\\dagger}`$ . One execution returns $`-2(-1)^{b_{c}+b_{t}}\\mathbf{e}_{\\widehat{r}}`$ for nodes $`4,5,6,7`$ .",
      "source_id": "A1.SS8.fig2",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS8.fig2"
    },
    {
      "kind": "Figure",
      "label": "Figure 9",
      "description": "Complex-Hopf magnitude checkpoint at depth $`2`$ . The inner and outer forward brackets use the real and complex state-column contracts. The reverse bracket uses the active-interface identity $`B_{2,\\mathbb{C}}P_{2}=D_{\\mathrm{ph}}B_{2}P_{2}`$ , which gives the same checkpoint correlators as the separated physical sequence $`D_{\\mathrm{ph}}^{\\dagger}`$ followed by $`B_{2}^{\\dagger}`$ . The readout measures $`q_{4}q_{3}`$ in $`Z`$ and $`q_{2}`$ together with the ancilla in $`Y`$ , while $`q_{1}`$ is discarded because it belongs to the unused lower suffix.",
      "source_id": "A1.SS8.SSS1.fig1",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS8.SSS1.fig1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "Table",
      "label": "Table III",
      "description": "Four-qubit compiler and record charges under the same direct-angle $`C_{\\mathrm{H}}`$ functional used in the theorem; the controlled observable and readout are excluded by definition. The theorem uses the designated separated schedules, while this table evaluates native-forward and integrated reverse alternatives whose finite constant ratios need not be $`2`$ and $`1`$ .",
      "source_id": "A1.SS9.fig1",
      "target": "science/four-qubit-example.md#A1.SS9.fig1"
    },
